# Grep Check Registry — nextjs-react pack fragment
#
# React / Next.js / TypeScript checks. The Phase 3 installer
# concatenates this fragment with core/companion/checks.yml.fragment
# (and any other selected pack fragments) into the project-side
# .claude/checks.yml. Path globs use placeholder vocabulary
# that the installer substitutes per project from convention_map.md.
#
# Helper names referenced in patterns and descriptions
# (e.g., getDisplayError, useAbortableFetch, BrandingControl.tsx)
# are placeholders for the equivalent helpers in the consuming
# project's convention_map.md. Override pattern strings post-install
# if your project uses different helper names.
#
# Format docs: WORKFLOW.md §6.5

checks:
  # ── XSS / link safety ──────────────────────────────────────────

  - id: window-open-noopener
    name: "window.open without noopener"
    category: security
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    include: ["*.tsx", "*.ts"]
    pattern: 'window\.open'
    negative_pattern: '([''"`])[^''"`\n]*(?<![-\w])noopener(?![-\w])[^''"`\n]*\1'
    notes: |
      `negative_pattern` requires `noopener` to sit INSIDE a string literal on
      the same line, bounded on both sides. The bare word suppressed a real
      finding whenever the token appeared anywhere on the line — including in a
      comment naming this very rule, whose id contains the substring, and in
      `"resizable,noopenerX"`.

      MEASURED HONESTLY. On the author's own 8 cases this scores 8/8, but that
      matrix was self-selected. An independent 32-case matrix scores the bare
      form wrong on 12 and this one wrong on 9 — a real improvement, not a
      clean win, and one of the 9 is a regression this change introduced.

      BOTH failure directions, because an earlier draft of this note named only
      the cheap one on a check whose own argument is that a missed call is
      worse than a waivable false positive:

      * FALSE POSITIVE (new, introduced here): a correct call whose features
        string embeds a quote — `window.open(u,"_blank",'w=5,noopener,t="x"')`
        — is flagged, because the character class cannot reach the closing
        delimiter past the inner quote.
      * FALSE POSITIVE (pre-existing, per-line): a `window.open(` whose
        arguments wrap onto later lines is reported on its opening line.
      * FALSE NEGATIVE: any *other* quoted `noopener` on the same line clears
        the call — a JSX `rel="noopener"` beside an inline handler, a URL
        containing the word, a nearby options object. A per-line regex cannot
        tell an argument of THIS call from a string elsewhere on the line.

      The false-negative class is why the per-call semgrep rule is the real
      fix. It LANDED in Phase 217 as `semgrep-window-open-noopener`
      (`Q-262` resolved), so this check is now the line-shaped first layer
      and that rule is the call-shaped one. This check stays
      `blocking: true` because the semgrep stage needs a binary a
      consumer's CI may not install; its twin is `blocking: false` for
      exactly that reason, so the two are not redundant.

      KNOWN LIMIT, stated rather than left to be discovered: this check is
      per-line, so a `window.open(` whose arguments wrap onto later lines is
      reported on its opening line even when a later line passes `noopener`.
      Running it file-level (`invert_file_check: true`) would remove that false
      positive and introduce a worse false negative — one correct call anywhere
      in a file would clear every other `window.open` in it, and a file of
      deliberately-bad calls measured CLEAN. Per-line is the deliberate choice:
      on a security check, a false positive a reviewer can waive beats a missed
      call nobody sees.

      To waive the wrapped-call case, put the marker on the line the finding
      CITES — the `window.open(` opening line, as a trailing comment:
      `window.open(  // no-check: window-open-noopener`. A marker on the line
      ABOVE does not waive anything; the runner only reads the reported line.
      An earlier draft of this note said "waive it with `// no-check:`" without
      saying where, and the natural placement for a wrapped call is the one
      that does not work.
    description: "window.open must include noopener,noreferrer in features string"
    convention: "window.open"
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: true

  - id: semgrep-window-open-noopener
    name: "window.open without noopener in the features string (semgrep)"
    category: security
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    description: "`window.open(...)` whose features argument is missing or does not contain `noopener`. Covers the 1-arg and 2-arg forms (no features string at all) and the 3-arg form whose features string omits noopener. Pass 'noopener,noreferrer' so the opened document gets no `window.opener` handle back to this origin."
    convention: "window.open"
    notes: |
      PRECISION LAYER for the grep twin `window-open-noopener` above,
      backported in Phase 217 (Q-262). **Read the twin's own `notes:` first —
      the gap this closes is the one they already name.** Sysop's twin is
      per-LINE, so a `window.open(` whose arguments are wrapped onto following
      lines puts the features string on a different line from the call: the
      twin's `negative_pattern` cannot see it, the call reports as a blocking
      MEDIUM finding, and the documented remedy is a hand-placed
      `// no-check:` marker on the opening line. This rule reads the AST and
      judges the call on its actual features argument, so the wrapped-but-safe
      shape is simply not a finding.

      Do NOT describe this as fixing a whole-file check. The extraction source's
      twin is a file-level invert check and its rule carries that rationale;
      Sysop's is per-line and the failure mode is different. Copying the
      upstream sentence here would have shipped a false claim about this pack.

      Rule YAML: `semgrep/window_open_noopener.yaml`; fixtures
      `semgrep/fixtures/window_open_noopener_{positive,negative}.ts`. The
      negative fixture includes a multi-line CORRECT call — the exact shape that
      false-positives under the per-line twin — and a comment containing the
      word `noopener`, which must not change any verdict. Proven on both:
      every positive case flagged and every negative case silent — the negative
      fixture is not empty, which is the half that matters: it carries a
      multi-line CORRECT call, three safe feature strings, and a comment
      containing the word `noopener`, and none of them may fire.

      `blocking: false` because a consumer's required CI job installs no semgrep
      binary — this rule runs locally and via the post-tool hook only, and a
      blocking semgrep check would record `skipped: tool-missing` and gate
      nothing. Promote per the graduation path in
      `core/companion/semgrep/README.md` only after semgrep runs in your CI.

      Measured before adoption against a real 205-file `.tsx` consumer frontend:
      0 findings, with the two live `window.open` call sites both correctly NOT
      flagged (both pass 'noopener,noreferrer').
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: false

  - id: dangerous-inner-html
    name: "dangerouslySetInnerHTML"
    category: security
    severity: critical
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    include: ["*.tsx", "*.ts"]
    pattern: 'dangerouslySetInnerHTML'
    description: "dangerouslySetInnerHTML usage detected — XSS risk"
    convention: "XSS prevention"
    used_by: [security-audit]

  # ── Error display ──────────────────────────────────────────────

  - id: error-display-jsx
    name: "Raw err.message in JSX"
    category: convention
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<components dir>/", "<hooks dir>/"]
    include: ["*.tsx", "*.ts"]
    exclude: ["*__tests__*", "*.d.ts"]
    pattern: '(err\.message|error\.message)'
    negative_pattern: 'getDisplayError'
    description: "Raw error.message rendered in UI — use getDisplayError() from lib/errorMessages.ts"
    convention: "Error display"
    notes: "Needs LLM triage: only flag if rendered in UI, not just logged or thrown"
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  # ── URL handling / fetch hygiene ───────────────────────────────

  - id: missing-encode-uri
    name: "Missing encodeURIComponent in Fetch URLs"
    category: convention
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<hooks dir>/", "<components dir>/"]
    include: ["*.ts", "*.tsx"]
    pattern: 'fetch\(`'
    negative_pattern: 'encodeURIComponent'
    description: "Template literal in fetch() without encodeURIComponent on dynamic path segments"
    convention: "URL encoding"
    notes: "Needs LLM triage: only flag if template interpolates dynamic values into URL path segments"
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  # ── Test conventions ───────────────────────────────────────────

  - id: to-be-defined-dom
    name: "toBeDefined() on DOM Queries"
    category: testing
    severity: low
    paths: ["<app dir>/"]
    include: ["*.ts", "*.tsx"]
    pattern: '\.toBeDefined\(\)'
    description: "toBeDefined() on DOM query is a no-op — use toBeInTheDocument() or not.toBeNull()"
    convention: "Assertion specificity"
    notes: "Needs LLM triage: only flag if preceded by querySelector, getByText, getByRole, or similar DOM queries"
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  - id: missing-mock-cleanup
    name: "Missing Mock Cleanup"
    category: testing
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<app dir>/"]
    include: ["*.ts", "*.tsx"]
    pattern: 'vi\.(mock|spyOn)\('
    negative_pattern: 'restoreAllMocks'
    invert_file_check: true
    description: "Test file uses vi.mock/vi.spyOn without afterEach(vi.restoreAllMocks())"
    convention: "Mock cleanup"
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  # ── Memory hygiene ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  - id: createobjecturl-leak
    name: "createObjectURL without revokeObjectURL"
    category: correctness
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<app dir>/"]
    include: ["*.ts", "*.tsx"]
    exclude: ["*__tests__*", "*.test.ts", "*.test.tsx"]
    pattern: 'URL\.createObjectURL\('
    negative_pattern: 'URL\.revokeObjectURL\('
    invert_file_check: true
    description: "URL.createObjectURL() without paired URL.revokeObjectURL() in same file — blob stays pinned in memory indefinitely. See 'Object URL revocation' convention; canonical pattern in BrandingControl.tsx."
    convention: "Object URL revocation"
    notes: "Files that delegate revocation to a helper imported from another module would FP. None today; manual triage needed if a future refactor extracts URL lifecycle into a util."
    used_by: [codebase-review]
    blocking: false

  # ── LSP / tsc diagnostics ──────────────────────────────────────
  # No `pattern` field — populated by run_lsp_diagnostics() in
  # sysop/scripts/run_checks/lsp.py. Starts blocking: false because CI
  # does not run `npm ci` before invoking run_checks.sh.

  - id: tsc-type-error
    name: "TypeScript type error (tsc --noEmit)"
    category: correctness
    severity: high
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    description: "tsc --noEmit reports a type error"
    convention: "Typechecker: TypeScript"
    used_by: [codebase-review]
    blocking: false

  # ── Semgrep / AST diagnostics ──────────────────────────────────
  # No `pattern` field — populated by `_run_semgrep()` in
  # sysop/scripts/run_checks/semgrep.py from the pack's semgrep/*.yaml rules.

  - id: semgrep-error-display-jsx
    name: "Raw error.message in JSX (semgrep)"
    category: security
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    description: "err.message / error.message rendered directly in JSX"
    convention: "Error display"
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: false

  - id: semgrep-missing-encode-uri
    name: "Missing encodeURIComponent in fetch() (semgrep)"
    category: security
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    description: "fetch() template literal interpolation without encodeURIComponent"
    convention: "URL encoding"
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: false

  - id: semgrep-abort-error-setstate
    name: "AbortError setState in useEffect (semgrep)"
    category: correctness
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    description: "setState in isAbortError() branch inside useEffect with cancelled guard — races next mount/run"
    convention: "AbortError loading state cleanup"
    used_by: [codebase-review]
    blocking: false

  - id: semgrep-missing-fetch-redirect
    name: "Missing redirect: 'error' in fetch() (semgrep)"
    category: security
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<frontend>/"]
    # Test directories are excluded because a mocked fetch has no redirect
    # surface: `global.fetch = vi.fn()` and friends are assignments, not real
    # requests, so every hit inside `__tests__/` is a false positive by
    # construction. It is here rather than in `paths:` for the same reason as
    # the python pack's `hot-path-regex` precedent — an unlocalized
    # `<frontend>/` placeholder is stripped as "not yet localized", so a fresh
    # install scans whole-tree, while `exclude_dir:` survives that strip and
    # narrows every stage uniformly.
    #
    # Measured through the real stage against a 205-file `.tsx` consumer
    # frontend carrying 178 test files (Phase 217): 8 findings before, ALL 8
    # inside `__tests__/`, 0 outside — 0 findings after, with no true positive
    # lost. That consumer had already been forced to add this exact key in its
    # own overlay, recording "8 findings, all false positives"; the divergence
    # was invisible upstream until the maintainer-side registry-drift tooling
    # (which never ships) learned to compare `exclude_dir` at all.
    #
    # The rule's own YAML says path scoping is declared in this fragment, so
    # shipping the fragment without it made that sentence false.
    # If you override this check in `.claude/checks.project.yml`, your entry
    # REPLACES this one wholesale (the collision merge is not a deep merge), so
    # copy `exclude_dir` across or the test-path false positives come back.
    exclude_dir: ["__tests__"]
    description: "fetch() options literal does not include redirect: 'error' — client-side fetches with Authorization headers can leak via open redirects to attacker-controlled hosts. Pre-set in useAbortableFetch wrapper for hook consumers."
    convention: "Fetch redirect guard"
    notes: "Variable-passed options (const opts = {...}; fetch(url, opts)) will FP. None today; manual triage if introduced. Test directories are excluded via exclude_dir (Phase 217) — a mocked fetch has no redirect surface; measured 8 -> 0 on a real consumer frontend with no true positive lost."
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: false
